wifi_drv_set_wds_sta(): re-enslave an already-existing WDS STA
interface into the bridge.

The AP_VLAN netdev that carries a 4-address/WDS station's traffic
(<vap>.sta<aid>, e.g. wifi1.1.sta1) is only added to the bridge in the
branch that creates it:

    if (!if_nametoindex(name)) {
        ... wlan_nl80211_create_interface(...) ...
        if (bridge_ifname && nl80211_create_bridge(name, bridge_ifname) != 0) { ... }
        ...
    } else {
        wifi_hal_dbg_print("Re-Enabling interface:%s - wds:%d\n", ...);
        nl80211_interface_enable(name, true);      /* no enslavement */
    }

So whenever the interface still exists from an earlier association, it
is brought up but never becomes a bridge port again, and the station
associates to an AP that cannot pass any of its traffic.

The teardown path does normally delete the interface
(nl80211_remove_from_bridge() + nl80211_delete_interface() when val==0),
which is why this is invisible in the common case. It stops being the
common case as soon as the AP side goes away without running that path
-- a OneWifi restart, a crash, or, in the containerised setup here, the
whole container being recreated: the mac80211_hwsim phy is passed
through to the container, so interfaces created on it outlive the
container's lifetime and the AP_VLAN netdev is still present when the
next association arrives.

Observed exactly that on the EasyMesh controller: wifi1.1.sta1 present,
absent from /sys/class/net/brlan0/brif, and "iw dev wifi1.1 station
dump" empty because the leaf had given up. The leaf associated, the
4-Way Handshake completed, and then AP-Autoconfiguration timed out with
no reply -- its 1905 frames had nowhere to go. Enslaving the interface
by hand made the leaf receive WSC M2 immediately (analyze_m2ctrl count
0 -> 7) and bring its fronthaul VAPs up, which is what identified this.

Failure is logged rather than returned, unlike the creation branch: at
this point the station is already associated and nl80211_set_sta_vlan()
below still needs to run, so aborting here would leave things in a worse
state than continuing with a diagnosable error.

Note this also picks up the operstate handling in nl80211_create_bridge()
(see 0014), so the re-added port is left forwarding rather than dormant.

---
 wifi_hal_nl80211.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/wifi_hal_nl80211.c b/wifi_hal_nl80211.c
--- a/wifi_hal_nl80211.c
+++ b/wifi_hal_nl80211.c
@@ -14687,6 +14687,14 @@
             wifi_hal_dbg_print("%s:%d:Re-Enabling interface:%s - wds:%d\n",
                 __func__, __LINE__, name, interface->u.ap.conf.wds_sta);
             nl80211_interface_enable(name, true);
+            /* The AP_VLAN netdev survived from an earlier association, but its bridge
+             * membership did not necessarily survive with it -- so re-assert it here.
+             * Enslaving only in the creation branch above leaves a re-associating station
+             * with no data path at all. */
+            if (bridge_ifname && nl80211_create_bridge(name, bridge_ifname) != 0) {
+                wifi_hal_error_print("%s:%d: interface:%s failed to re-add to bridge:%s\n",
+                    __func__, __LINE__, name, bridge_ifname);
+            }
         }
         return nl80211_set_sta_vlan(radio, interface, addr, name, 0, link_id);
     } else {
